</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b51997c4-7a1c-11f1-a318-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-07T145201Z-1737568218-RC229MAKBIJX-RTRMADP-3-NATO-SUMMIT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b51997c4-7a1c-11f1-a318-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-07T145201Z-1737568218-RC229MAKBIJX-RTRMADP-3-NATO-SUMMIT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f971edfc-7a91-11f1-a752-2d0dd7f2e273</guid><pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 06:11:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b51997c4-7a1c-11f1-a318-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-07T145201Z-1737568218-RC229MAKBIJX-RTRMADP-3-NATO-SUMMIT.jpg">© Yves Herman / Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>TotalEnergies to discuss Syria offshore exploration, still wary of insecurity</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260708-totalenergies-to-discuss-syria-offshore-exploration-still-wary-of-insecurity</link><description>
TotalEnergies CEO Patrick Pouyanné said security concerns still rule out a return to onshore oil operations, but confirmed the French company is rebuilding its Iraq-Syria oil transit routes. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/186b98ae-7a13-11f1-8c5d-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Macron-in-SYRIA-7-july-2026-rtr.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/186b98ae-7a13-11f1-8c5d-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Macron-in-SYRIA-7-july-2026-rtr.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">08e48de8-7a11-11f1-878d-f7f8cee5d467</guid><pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 05:05:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/186b98ae-7a13-11f1-8c5d-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Macron-in-SYRIA-7-july-2026-rtr.jpg">REUTERS - Mahmoud Hassano</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>Bombs injure 18 in Damascus as Macron makes historic visit to Syria</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260707-macron-makes-historic-visit-to-syria-despite-security-concerns</link><description>
Two devices were detonated close to the hotel where French President Emmanuel Macron is staying during his visit to Damascus – the first by a European head of state since Bashar al-Assad's regime fell in 2024.
